# Three hell hounds, a hundred turns of their fire, and the suit's page either
# side. Included by quality-self-immune-plain.keys and
# quality-self-immune-quality.keys, AFTER each of those has or has not put the
# quality on the suit.
# Read by tools/check_quality_self_immune.sh (bd inc-to9x).
#
# NOT THE FLAME DRAKE, because it cannot be summoned at all. It is declared
# MA_REPTILIAN only (lib/mon1.irh:1474), MA_REPTILIAN is 83
# (inc/Defines.h:1618), and the summon menu walks 1..MA_LAST_REAL, which is 74
# (src/Term.cpp:2887). No drake appears in any category.
#
# WHY HELL HOUNDS. Three things matter, and the hound (lib/mon4.irh:1992) is the
# only summonable fire-breather that has all three.
#   - It knows no spells. A monster adds its breath to the action list at
#     P_URGENT (src/Monster.cpp:881), but a caster spends most of its turns
#     casting instead; a red dragon was measured breathing four times in a
#     hundred turns for exactly that reason, and a fire mephit fights other
#     mephits.
#   - Its breath is 2d6 (lib/mon4.irh:1999), and it lands as the 2d6 it
#     declares. It used to land as 1d6: src/Fight.cpp assigned
#     max(1,e.Dmg.Number) over the dice the statblock had just declared, so
#     EVERY monster breath in this game delivered ONE die. inc-19ay made that
#     max a floor under the declared count instead of a replacement of it, and
#     the hound now breathes what it says it breathes. The item takes half of
#     that.
#   - It is M_GROUP, so three of them hunt together instead of fighting each
#     other, and three breaths a round is what makes a hundred turns enough.
#
# THE ORACLE is the suit's own page, read before and after. Its hit points are
# the behavioural half; its hardness table, which ItemHardnessDesc builds by
# calling Item::Hardness once per damage type, is the direct half. The game's
# combat-numbers line (src/Item.cpp:1511-1531) prints "vs. 0 (leather)" or
# "vs. Immune (leather)" as it happens, but only the LAST such line survives to
# a screen dump, so no assertion rests on catching one.
#
# WHY A HUNDRED TURNS. The equipment block reaches the armour on about one
# breath in four. Measured on seed 5 after inc-19ay, a hundred turns takes the
# unprotected suit from 56 hit points to 43. Measured on the same seed before
# inc-19ay, when every breath delivered one die instead of its declared two,
# the same hundred turns took it from 56 to 52.

@until "SPACE:Swap" i
@until ">>j)Armour" j
@until "base value of" x
@dump:burn-before
@until "Things in View" ESC

w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Dogs"
@choose "hell hound"
RIGHT*3 ENTER
@while "more --" SPACE
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Dogs"
@choose "hell hound"
LEFT*3 ENTER
@while "more --" SPACE
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Dogs"
@choose "hell hound"
UP*2 ENTER
@while "more --" SPACE
@dump:burn-summoned

